#
# Security tier: hardened
#
# Everything built, and STRICT=2: refuse where the default sanitises, and
# tighten what a careful operator would want tightened even though ordinary
# use never arrives there. A script that meets one of those refusals was
# meant to. The kernel is every region at its hardened level: the allocator
# keeps its caches apart and zeroes what it hands out, indirect branch
# tracking and the strongest mitigations are on, root cannot write the
# kernel's memory (integrity lockdown), a device cannot read all of it (strict
# IOMMU). See sec_default for the tiers and how to pick one.
#
#   sh build.sh debug_none limbo desktop wifi serial sec_hardened
#

#> includes sec_baseline sec_mem_hardened sec_cpu_hardened sec_surface_hardened sec_dma_hardened sec_lockdown_hardened sec_build_hardened

CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y
CONFIG_INTEGRITY=n

CONFIG_MOONWATER_STRICT=2

#
# Floodlight: every dangerous-flag switch off, which is the denials the
# default refused before 2026-09-28 (awk starting programs with AWK_SPAWN,
# script and setarch starting a shell with SHELL_ESCAPES) and the rest of the
# bundles, and the policy sealed at boot so no later line to /dev/floodlight
# loosens it. Each switch's help in src/moonwater/Kconfig names the exploit
# class it closes. The POLICY string stays empty: the rows it once spelled
# are the first two switches' own.
#
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_SEAL=y
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_AWK_SPAWN=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_SHELL_ESCAPES=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_FIND_EXEC=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_FIND_DELETE=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_FIND_WRITE=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_XARGS=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_SPLIT_FILTER=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_SORT_COMPRESS=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_ENV_SPLIT=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_LAUNCHERS=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_NAMESPACES=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_NETWORK_FETCH=n
